{
  "dom_id": "step-q105122",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14105016/assets/80b80ea901c031439e50f42b34146401c75c65f9947ecca75ee8afda94540465.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(4)\\ne f(8)$\n- $\u2200\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is not an injection",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $\u2200\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the diagram, we have $f(4)=1$ (we have an arrow from $4\\in X$ to $1\\in Y$) and $f(8)=1$ (we have an arrow from $8\\in X$ to $1\\in Y$).\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the diagram, $f(4)=f(8)$ but $4\\ne 8.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
